---
// Who maintains Agent Codeyard, why it exists and what it is built on, in the
// first screen (#319); then the reasons in more detail, what it can do now and
// what it cannot, its design decisions, one paragraph on its history, the open
// work, and the source and licences. The history period by period, the
// principles it taught and the research moved to /docs/history/. From the
// earlier site's pages (commit 6a62f84), corrected as the audit of #235 lists;
// the limits follow the docs (configuration-and-security,
// new-to-agentic-coding) and the open Issues; Codeyard's own terms are
// explained at their first use.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Table from "../components/Table.astro";
import { site } from "../../site.config";
import { href, issue, pull, repoFile } from "../lib/links";

// Possible improvements, statuses as the audit of #235 corrected them (items 8, 10–13, 15).
const improvements = [
  { name: "Separate agent identity", what: "A GitHub identity with limited rights for the agents, so an approval is one the agent cannot give itself", status: "Not built", ref: 99 },
  { name: "GitHub approval rules", what: "A ruleset, required checks and code-owner review that GitHub enforces on the merge", status: "Available once you configure them; doctor.sh warns when the ruleset is missing" },
  { name: "Second reviewer", what: "One review of the behaviour and one of the engineering quality", status: "Not built", ref: 66 },
  { name: "Periodic run analysis", what: "The analysis of feature runs on a schedule", status: "Not built: analyze-runs.sh runs when you start it", ref: 131 },
  { name: "Selective CI", what: "Running the tests a change affects", status: "Partly: check groups and partial checks in repair rounds" },
  { name: "Model selection by cost", what: "Choosing a model by what a task needs and what it costs", status: "Partly: a model per role and risk level" },
  { name: "Cost of runs", what: "Duration, retries, models and cost per run", status: "Partly: durations, retries and tokens; no cost in money" },
  { name: "Parallel features", what: "Dependencies automation can use, and agents working on several features at once", status: "Not built: separate worktrees allow concurrent work that you start" },
  { name: "Environment isolation", what: "Per task its own database, services, ports and credentials", status: "Not built: a worktree separates only the working files" },
  { name: "Browser validation", what: "Browser tests with screenshots or traces for interface changes", status: "Not built: a project can configure its own" },
  { name: "Built-in remote runs, a dashboard, several repositories", what: "Starting and watching runs on another machine from Codeyard, a browser view of them, work across repositories", status: "Not built: you can run the scripts yourself on a server, as the maintainer does (Examples)" },
];
---
<Base title="About" description="Who maintains Agent Codeyard, why it exists and what it is built on; what it can and cannot do today, its design decisions and the open work.">
  <PageHead title="About">
    <dl class="answers">
      <div><dt>Who</dt><dd class="lede">{site.name} is an open-source project by Ahmet Taspinar, founder of <a href="https://jidai.nl/">JIDAI</a>, who maintains it and merges its pull requests.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Why</dt><dd>Coding agents can plan, build, test and review a feature, but they do not show you that the review and the tests saw the code you merge, or keep the decisions that matter with you. Codeyard applies established engineering practice to that.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Built on</dt><dd>Claude Code and Codex write the code. Codeyard is the workflow around them, in shell scripts and plain files in your repository, with no platform to host. It is built with its own workflow, as <a href={href("examples/")}>Examples</a> shows.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <ol class="contents">
      <li><a href="#why">Why Codeyard exists</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">What it can do now, and what it cannot</a></li>
      <li><a href="#decisions">Design decisions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#history">History and research</a></li>
      <li><a href="#improvements">Possible improvements</a></li>
      <li><a href="#contribute">Source, licence and contributing</a></li>
    </ol>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="why" title="Why Codeyard exists">
    <p class="lede">How do you keep the requirements consistent, know that a change was reviewed and tested on the code that is merged, and let agents work on their own without giving up the decisions that matter?</p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li><b>Clear requirements reduce rework.</b> Every plan starts with a round of questions and your approval, and every feature with the implementer's check that the Issue settles what it needs.</li>
      <li><b>Independent reviews catch what the author misses.</b> By default every feature is reviewed by a read-only agent of another provider before the commit, and every finding gets a decision: fix, defer, or accept with a reason. A project can let low-risk features skip the review; their merge then needs the owner.</li>
      <li><b>Approval does not rest on an agent's judgement alone.</b> Rules about what a change touches come first; the reviewer's assessment can only make the outcome stricter.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" title="What it can do now, and what it cannot">
    <Fragment slot="note">The limits as the docs state them: <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/")}>Configuration and security</a>. Short answers: the <a href={href("faq/")}>FAQ</a>.</Fragment>
    <div class="split">
      <div><h3>It can</h3><ul>
        <li>Start a new project with <code>gh repo create --template</code> and plan it with you: requirements, architecture and roadmap, reviewed by another provider and approved by you</li>
        <li>Take each feature from its Issue to an open pull request, step by step or unattended, on its own branch in its own worktree, a second working folder of the same repository</li>
        <li>Compute a risk level for each change from the files it touches, and scale the review and the models to it</li>
        <li>Refuse a reviewer of the implementer's own provider, and run every reviewer read-only</li>
        <li>Commit only when the checks passed and the implementer's completion record, its account of each acceptance criterion with what shows it, says the work is complete</li>
        <li>Mark a pull request that needs your approval: a protected file, a major or breaking impact on the architecture, or no review</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div><h3>It cannot, today</h3><ul>
        <li>Take an existing repository's architecture as settled when it adopts the workflow: the assessment reads the files and proposes a provisional reconstruction of the architecture, requirements and roadmap, marked <em>observed, not approved</em> until you approve the planning (<a href={href("docs/adopting/")}>Adopting</a>)</li>
        <li>Stop an agent from merging: no script merges, but an agent has the GitHub rights of the account that started it until it gets an identity of its own (<a href={issue(99)}>#99</a>). Only the settings of <a href={href("docs/github-setup/")}>GitHub setup</a> make GitHub refuse a merge</li>
        <li>Isolate the agents: they run on your machine, as you, under their own tools' sandbox settings; a worktree is not a security boundary</li>
        <li>Run other coding agents than Claude Code and Codex, tell you a cost in money, or start, watch and orchestrate runs remotely: the scripts run where you start them, on your machine or a server of yours, as the <a href={href("examples/#story")}>runs on the maintainer's server</a> show</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="decisions" title="Design decisions">
    <Fragment slot="note">Each with the pull request that made it.</Fragment>
    <Table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">decision</th><th scope="col">why</th><th scope="col">source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Read-only reviewers</th><td data-col="why">A reviewer that can edit can fix what it should report.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(35)}>PR #35</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A reviewer of another provider</th><td data-col="why">An agent of the same family shares the implementer's blind spots.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(211)}>PR #211</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Evidence tied to content</th><td data-col="why">A review or a verification counts only for the content it saw.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(42)}>#42</a>, <a href={pull(74)}>#74</a>, <a href={pull(200)}>#200</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Scripts decide outcomes</th><td data-col="why">An unattended run needs a judge that does not depend on how a session ended.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(101)}>PR #101</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">The completion gate</th><td data-col="why">A session that ends is not a finished feature.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(126)}>PR #126</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Your merge</th><td data-col="why">No script merges; a protected change asks for the owner's approval, which only GitHub's settings enforce.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(100)}>PR #100</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A computed risk level</th><td data-col="why">Read from the diff and the base branch's rules, so a feature cannot classify itself lower.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(194)}>PR #194</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Local run records</th><td data-col="why">Measurement without sending anything anywhere.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(136)}>PR #136</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Issues when ready</th><td data-col="why">The roadmap is not turned into a backlog in advance.</td><td data-col="source"><a href={pull(44)}>PR #44</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </Table>
  </Section>

  <Section id="history" title="History and research">
    <p>Codeyard started on 10 September 2026 as a manual workflow and automated the steps that proved useful: recorded reviews and verification first, then unattended runs, records of every run, and effort by risk. Until 31 October it was called {site.formerName}; the repository became <a href={site.repository}>{site.repository.replace(/^https:\/\/github\.com\//, "")}</a> on 2 November (<a href={pull(268)}>PR #268</a>). <a href={href("docs/history/")}>History and research</a> follows each period with its pull requests, the principles it taught, and the <a id="research" href={href("docs/history/#research")}>frameworks and talks</a> it learned from.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="improvements" title="Possible improvements">
    <Fragment slot="note">Not a committed roadmap. The open work is in the <a href={`${site.repository}/issues`}>Issues</a>.</Fragment>
    <Table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">improvement</th><th scope="col">what it would add</th><th scope="col">status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {improvements.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
            <td data-col="what it would add">{row.what}</td>
            <td data-col="status">{row.status}{row.ref && <> (<a href={issue(row.ref)}>#{row.ref}</a>)</>}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p class="needs">Built since the earlier list of this site: feature clarification (<a href={pull(213)}>PR #213</a>), a model per risk level, check groups, partial checks and budgets.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="contribute" title="Source, licence and contributing">
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>maintainer</dt><dd>Ahmet Taspinar, founder of <a href="https://jidai.nl/">JIDAI</a></dd>
      <dt>source</dt><dd><a href={site.repository}>{site.repository.replace(/^https:\/\//, "")}</a></dd>
      <dt>licence</dt><dd>MIT, in <a href={repoFile("LICENSE")}><code>LICENSE</code></a>. A project created with <code>gh repo create --template</code> chooses its own licence. The fonts of this site are under the SIL Open Font License: <a href={href("fonts/OFL.txt")}>Font licence</a>.</dd>
      <dt>contributing</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("CONTRIBUTING.md")}><code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code></a>: changes start from an Issue, on a feature branch, following <a href={repoFile("AGENTS.md")}><code>AGENTS.md</code></a></dd>
      <dt>security</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("SECURITY.md")}><code>SECURITY.md</code></a>: report security-sensitive findings privately</dd>
    </dl>
  </Section>
</Base>
